---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import CompareTable from "../../components/CompareTable.astro";
import CtaBand from "../../components/CtaBand.astro";

const rows = [
  { label: "Inserts extra invisible characters into your text", them: true, us: false },
  { label: "Flagged by scanners, linters and editor plugins", them: true, us: false },
  { label: "Survives copy-paste, scraping and reformatting", them: "Often lost in handling", us: true },
  { label: "Encodes which store the text belongs to", them: "DIY-dependent", us: true },
  { label: "Detection tool included", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Produces an ownership document for a takedown", them: false, us: true },
];
---

<Base
  title="Kirpik vs zero-width character tricks"
  description="Zero-width characters are a well-known DIY text fingerprinting trick, and a well-known thing to scan for and strip. Kirpik does not use zero-width characters. Here is the difference."
>
  <PageHero
    title="Zero-width characters vs an algorithmic watermark"
    lead="Search for DIY text fingerprinting and you will find the zero-width trick: sprinkle invisible Unicode characters through your copy and catch thieves who paste them along. It is a clever idea with a public weakness. Kirpik does not use it."
  />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2>The trick, as the blog posts tell it</h2>
      <p>
        Unicode includes characters with no visible width, designed for legitimate typography and text
        processing. The DIY fingerprinting recipe inserts them at chosen spots in a piece of text. The
        text looks unchanged, the pattern of insertions encodes a message, and if a copied paragraph
        shows up elsewhere with the pattern intact, you have your match. People have used variants of
        this to trace leaked documents and detect copy-paste plagiarism, and it does work, right up until
        it meets anyone or anything that has read the same blog posts.
      </p>

      <h2>The publicly documented weakness</h2>
      <p>
        Zero-width characters are famous, and famous is fatal for a hiding place. They are specific,
        known codepoints, which means they can be searched for by name:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Code editors and their plugins highlight zero-width characters, a practice that spread widely
          after they were shown off as a vehicle for hidden text and source-code fingerprinting.
        </li>
        <li>
          Security tooling and linters flag them, because invisible codepoints are also used in phishing
          and obfuscation attacks.
        </li>
        <li>
          Online "invisible character detectors" and cleaner tools remove them in one paste. So does a
          find and replace across a handful of well-known codepoints.
        </li>
        <li>
          Many platforms and editors normalise text on paste or save, quietly dropping the characters
          without anyone even trying. The fingerprint dissolves in ordinary handling.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        There is also a subtler problem: even when the characters survive, presence is all they show. A
        pile of zero-width codepoints in a paragraph does not, by itself, say whose paragraph it was,
        unless your DIY encoding also carries an identity and you can decode it under scrutiny.
      </p>

      <h2>What Kirpik does instead</h2>
      <p>
        Kirpik does not use zero-width characters. Its watermark is an algorithmic signature derived from
        your original text, embedded invisibly and tied to your store's unique watermark code, and we do
        not publish its internals, for the same reason a bank does not publish its vault schematics.
        What we do publish is what you can check from the outside: your words are not rewritten, nothing
        is visible to readers, search engines see your copy unchanged, and the signature survives
        copy-paste, scraping, trimming and light rewording.
      </p>
      <p>
        The other difference is that a watermark is only half the product. Kirpik pairs it with
        detection, paste a sample or check a URL and get back a match with a confidence figure, and with
        the Proof of Ownership Certificate, which turns a detection into a dated, independently verifiable
        ownership document. A DIY fingerprint leaves you doing all of that yourself, with a technique the
        whole internet knows how to strip.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <CompareTable themLabel="Zero-width trick" rows={rows} />
      <p class="tnote">
        The zero-width column describes the DIY technique as publicly documented in blog posts, editor
        plugins and detector tools; behaviour varies with each implementation. Kirpik's statements are
        limited to what it does not use and to externally checkable properties. Not legal advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="A hiding place everyone knows is not a hiding place."
    sub="Kirpik's watermark is algorithmic, invisible, and built to survive what actually happens to stolen text. Free to start."
  />
</Base>

<style>
  .tnote { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--ink-faint); line-height: 1.65; }
</style>
